-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261111.1~sparky9~0 (2026-11-11)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 521: Reconcile inherited /tmp tmpfs fstab entries when tmp.mount is masked.
    Keep the original as a backup and comment only the conflicting line.
    A required masked mount previously prevented local-fs.target startup,
    intermittently leaving the EFI partition unmounted. Never mount or unmount
    anything during migration; preserve administrator units and unmasked choices.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261108.1~sparky9~0 (2026-11-08)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * ORDER 463: the generator the old edition's automatic dependency left (systemd-zram-generator, marked automatic, not
    chosen) is purged once, as thermald was settled (ORDER 43): sparky-stereo-kernel-settings-zram-leftover.service waits
    until no apt or dpkg run holds the locks, simulates the purge, and purges only when nothing else installed needs it;
    started without waiting by the postinst, and at boot while the leftover is still there. A generator the person chose
    (apt-mark manual, Stereo APTus, ZRAM=on in the old file, zram-tools by hand) is kept as it is, and the guard
    (--allow) still holds it off until it is gone. The notice no longer says the leftover "is not used" (Debian's
    /usr/lib/systemd/zram-generator.conf makes the generator create zram0 at every boot): it says the generator is being
    removed, and a second message says it was
  * A failed systemd-zram-setup@zram0 is told by a handler of this package with no Repair: the engine's generic handler
    (Repair: systemctl restart) is masked by name for that unit, because it offered to start a unit the edition did not set up
  * tests/leftover.sh (red on 20261106.1, green on this version), in the systemd container

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 20261106.1~sparky9~0 (2026-11-06)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Ship /etc/tmpfiles.d/tmp.conf as a conffile: "q /tmp 1777 root root -", so
    /tmp keeps its files on every install (no boot wipe, no age cleanup);
    /var/tmp keeps Debian's 30 days. It overrides Debian's file of the same name.
  * tests/tmp-on-disk-test.sh checks the effective rule, that a clean pass removes
    nothing from /tmp aged 11 days, and that an edited conffile survives an upgrade.

